  "worked_solution": "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=27,$ $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $N=5.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}27{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1} & =27\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =27\\cdot \\frac{[1-\\frac{1}{243}]}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =27\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{242}{243})}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =27\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{242}{243} \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
